/BipartiteGraph\/findOddCycle()","abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"An odd cycle, or nil when the graph is bipartite (Boost "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"find_odd_cycle"},{"type":"text","text":"): the first one a"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"breadth-first two-colouring meets (roots in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertices"},{"type":"text","text":" order, rows in "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"incidentEdges"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order), closed through the conflicting edge’s ends’ lowest common ancestor in the search"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"tree. A self-loop is an odd cycle of length 1. In Cycles’ canonical form: it starts at its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"least vertex and leaves through the lesser of its two edges. Not necessarily a shortest odd"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"cycle. O(n + m)."}],"kind":"symbol","role":"symbol","type":"topic","url":"\/documentation\/bipartitegraphs\/bipartitegraph\/findoddcycle()","title":"findOddCycle()"},"doc://BipartiteGraphs/documentation/BipartiteGraphs/BipartiteGraph/bipartition()":{"identifier":"doc:\/\/BipartiteGraphs\/documentation\/BipartiteGraphs\/BipartiteGraph\/bipartition()","abstract":[{"text":"The two sides, or nil when the graph is not bipartite (","type":"text"},{"code":"findOddCycle()","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is then the witness).","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"In each connected component the least vertex (in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order) is left and the rest","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"alternate, so isolated vertices are left; each side lists its vertices in ","type":"text"},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" order","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"(igraph’s assignment). O(n + m).","type":"text"}],"url":"\/documentation\/bipartitegraphs\/bipartitegraph\/bipartitio